Easy Screen

Share a browser tab or window directly with other people, with no account, no install and no server of your own. In rooms created with "Create room (BETA)", several people can share their screen and turn on their camera in the same room, with the host's permission. The whole app is a single HTML file.

Live site: https://cross-creative.github.io/easy_screen/


How it works

Video travels directly from the host's browser to each viewer's browser (peer-to-peer, using WebRTC). A public PeerJS server (https://peerjs.com) is used only to help browsers find each other. No video passes through it, and nothing is stored.


Quick start

Sharing

1. Open the site on a computer.
2. Choose a nickname, pick a quality level and decide whether to share audio.
3. Click "Share screen" and select the tab or window to share.
4. Send the 6-character room code, or the link, to your viewers.

You can also click "Create room (BETA)" to open a room where several people can share their screens and turn on their cameras, with the host's permission. In rooms created with "Share screen", only the host shares, and there is no camera.

When you click to create a room, you first pick the room type and then choose the tab or window (no window is asked for when using "Create room (BETA)"). Public is the default: anyone with the link or code can join. Private: you get a notice with the person's nickname and accept or decline each one.

Watching

1. Open the link, or choose a nickname, type the room code on the home page and click "Join".
2. If the video starts muted because of browser autoplay rules, click the audio icon in the bar under the video and click "Unmute".
3. In rooms created with "Create room (BETA)", you can share your own screen: click "Ask to share" and, when the host allows it, click "Share". You can also show your camera: click "Ask to turn on camera" and, when the host allows it, click "Turn on camera". Screen and camera are separate permissions. In the other rooms only the host shares.


Features

Room codes: each host gets a random 6-character code, plus a shareable link that joins the room directly.

Nickname required to host or join. If the nickname is already taken, a dialog asks for another one instead of removing you from the room. The nickname is saved as you type and shown again after you leave a room. Opening a room link joins the room right away when a nickname is saved. Without one, you type the nickname and press Enter or click Join, and the code is already filled in.

The bar under the video shows "Host: name" on the left, with the Picture Mode, Fullscreen and Audio icons on the right. Hold the mouse over an icon for 1 second to see what it does. The host also sees a viewer counter like "3 👁".

Chat: users can chat with each other. You are responsible for what you say, and the host can mute you, delete your messages or kick you.

Host moderation: mute, kick and delete messages from the people panel. The panel also has buttons to revoke a person's screen permission or camera permission separately.

Temporary ban after a kick: 5 minutes by viewer identifier and 2 minutes by IP address.

Entry queue: to prevent floods, a room admits one new person every 5 seconds. People who arrive at the same time wait in an organized queue and see their position on screen. Up to 8 people can wait; beyond that the site shows that the room is busy. After a host handover, people who were already in the room reconnect without waiting.

Alternate room addresses: besides the main address, a room can be recovered on alternate addresses after a host handover. Viewers try the main address first and only try the alternates, one at a time, if the main one does not answer.

Automatic reconnection: if a viewer's connection drops, the site retries up to 8 times with increasing delays and keeps the chat on screen.

Tab or window sharing with optional audio.

Multi-sharing (only in rooms created with "Create room (BETA)"): viewers ask to share and the host allows, denies or revokes. Up to 4 people share their screen at the same time, host included.

Camera (only in rooms created with "Create room (BETA)"): video only, with no microphone. The host can turn on their own camera from the Options panel. Viewers ask to turn on the camera and the host allows, denies or revokes, separately from the screen permission. Up to 4 cameras at the same time, host included, counted apart from the 4 screens. A person can share screen and camera together, and phones can turn on the camera even though they cannot share a screen. The site uses the device's default camera, and your own camera preview is mirrored. The camera has its own quality selector in the Options panel (see "Quality levels and upload").

Grid with one tile per person. When someone shares both screen and camera, the tile shows the screen with the camera as a small square in the bottom right corner. Click the small square to swap them (camera large, screen small). Someone who only has the camera on appears with the camera at full size. Click a tile to see only that person, and use the button in its top right corner to go back to all tiles. Fullscreen then shows only that tile.

Audio icon in the bar under the video: opens a small panel. For viewers it has the volume slider and the mute button. For the host it has the button that mutes the audio being shared. When more than one person is sharing a screen, the panel also shows a volume slider and a mute button for each shared screen. This only affects your own device. Cameras have no audio.

Public and private rooms: in a private room the host accepts or declines each person who tries to join. Requests expire after 90 seconds, and people who were accepted can reconnect without asking again.

Quality levels from 480p up to QHD (1440p) for the screen, and from 360p up to 720p for the camera, adjustable while streaming.

Automatic quality reduction: when the browser reports that your upload or your computer is the bottleneck, the app drops one quality level by itself and shows a notice. It lowers the screen first. The camera only drops by itself when no screen is being shared. After 1 minute without problems the screen goes back up one level at a time, never above the quality you picked. Both behaviors are on by default and can be turned off in Settings.

Quality limit by number of viewers: the more people in the room, the lower the highest quality that can be selected. Locked levels are marked with a lock in the quality selectors (screen and camera), and the quality is lowered automatically if the room grows. When your camera is on, its upload counts toward the limit on the screen quality, and the other way around. This can be turned off in Settings. See "Quality levels and upload" for the exact limits.

Settings: the gear button in the corner of the screen opens the same panel on the home page, in the host room and in the viewer room. It has the language (Portuguese or English), the theme (automatic, light or dark), automatic quality reduction, automatic quality recovery and the quality limit by number of viewers and the system notifications. Settings are saved in your browser.

What's new notice: the home page shows a one-time notice about the settings. It disappears after you click "Got it" or open the settings, and does not come back.

Leave to menu: the host can go to the menu while the room stays open for 8 minutes, with a countdown shown to everyone, and come back with "Back to the room". Starting a new room closes the old one.

Warning for the host when upload bandwidth or CPU is overloaded, with a one-click option to lower the quality.

Update notice: every 5 minutes (and when you come back to the tab) the site checks whether a new version was published. If so, a notice asks everyone to restart the page. On the home page there is a "Restart now" button. Inside a room the notice asks you to close the room and reload the page, and can be dismissed. Nothing is sent during this check: the page only downloads its own file again and compares it.

Floating alerts: requests to join, share or turn on the camera, upload or CPU warnings and automatic quality changes appear as a notice fixed at the top of the window. It follows the screen when you scroll, so it stays visible on small screens.

System notifications: off by default. When you turn them on in Settings, the browser asks for permission. If the browser does not allow them (or the permission is blocked later), the option turns itself off and is disabled in Settings. When on, and when the room tab is in the background or the window is not focused, the same alerts also appear as a notification on your computer. Clicking one takes you back to the room tab.

Estimated upload usage and a live viewer counter. The estimate adds the screen and the camera.

Up to 16 viewers per room.

Stop and resume sharing, turn the camera on or off, or switch to another tab or window, without kicking anyone out of the room.

Options panel for the host. The small red "End room" button sits in the top row, next to Options and Chat, and asks for confirmation before ending the room.

Short messages (such as "Room ended") disappear on their own after 2 seconds, with the letters fading from right to left. Error messages stay on screen until you close them with the X button.

One tab per viewer per room: duplicate tabs from the same browser are blocked.

Fullscreen and Picture Mode (picture-in-picture) for both host and viewers, from the bar under the video, where the browser supports it. When more than one person is in the grid, you choose which tile goes to Picture Mode (the screen if the person has one, otherwise the camera).

Host recovery: if the host closes the tab, everyone in the room sees a notice with a 10-second countdown. If the host reopens the site in the same browser within 8 seconds, the home screen shows a "Back to the room" button with a countdown, and clicking it resumes the room with the same code, room type, sharing mode and permissions (screen and camera). Otherwise the person who joined the room first becomes the new host, keeping the same code and link. The new host keeps the room settings, the last 50 chat messages, the people muted, the temporary bans by viewer identifier and the screen and camera permissions. IP bans and any active sharing are not passed on: people who were sharing a screen or a camera need to turn it on again.

English and Portuguese (Brazil), detected from the browser, remembered and changeable in Settings.

Light and dark theme, following the system setting by default and changeable in Settings.


Quality levels and upload

Each viewer receives a separate copy of the video, so the host's upload grows with the audience. These are approximate values per viewer:

Screen
Very light: 480p, 10 fps, about 0.3 Mbps
Economy: 720p, 15 fps, about 0.8 Mbps
Balanced: 720p, 24 fps, about 1.5 Mbps
High: 1080p, 30 fps, about 3 Mbps
QHD: 1440p, 60 fps, about 8 Mbps

Camera
Light camera: 360p, 15 fps, about 0.3 Mbps
Normal camera: 480p, 24 fps, about 0.6 Mbps
HD camera: 720p, 30 fps, about 1.2 Mbps

Example: 4 viewers at Balanced need about 6 Mbps of upload. With the camera on at Normal, add about 2.4 Mbps. Anyone who shares uploads one copy of each stream to every other person in the room, so 4 people sharing in a room of 16 can need far more. The actual resolution also depends on the size of the shared tab or window and on what the camera supports.

When the quality limit by number of viewers is on, the app keeps viewers multiplied by the bitrate of the level at or below about 13 Mbps. For the screen, with no camera on, in practice:

1 viewer: up to QHD
2 to 4 viewers: up to High
5 to 8 viewers: up to Balanced
9 to 16 viewers: up to Economy

When the camera is on, its bitrate is added to each viewer before the calculation, so the limits above get lower. For the camera, the same calculation uses the screen bitrate (if a screen is being shared) plus the camera level.

The same calculation applies to anyone sharing from inside the room, using the number of people they send video to.


Browser support

Desktop Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari: can share a screen, turn on the camera and watch.
Android browsers: can watch and, in rooms with permission, turn on the camera. Cannot share the screen.
iOS browsers: can watch and, in rooms with permission, turn on the camera. Cannot share the screen.

Mobile browsers do not currently support screen capture, so on phones the site lets you watch and show the camera. Audio capture also depends on the browser: sharing a tab's audio works best in Chrome and Edge, and sharing a whole window's audio is limited. The camera needs the browser's permission to use the device, and a computer or phone without a camera cannot turn it on.


Self-hosting

Self-hosting a copy requires prior written permission from the author (see License).

With permission, only index.html is required. The optional icone.png is used as the page icon, and the page works without it.

GitHub Pages

1. Put index.html in the root of a repository.
2. In the repository settings, open Pages and select the branch to publish.

Netlify

Drag the folder containing index.html into https://app.netlify.com/drop

Both serve the site over HTTPS, which browsers require for screen capture and camera access.


Limitations

Mesh topology: everyone who shares uploads one stream per person in the room, and a screen plus a camera count as two streams. The limit is 16 viewers, but in practice it depends on the host's upload. It is best suited to small groups, roughly 3 to 6 people at higher quality levels.

Automatic quality reduction depends on what the browser reports about bandwidth and CPU limits, so it reacts after the problem has started and may not catch every case.

Floating alerts are not shown while a screen is in fullscreen. System notifications still work if the browser allows them.

The update notice can take up to 5 minutes to appear after a new version is published. A host who reloads the page can resume the room by clicking "Back to the room" within 8 seconds, and viewers who joined by link can reopen it. Viewers who typed the code need to type it again.

Depends on the free public PeerJS server, which can be slow or unavailable.

Viewers behind very restrictive networks, such as some school or company networks, may fail to connect.

If the host loses internet or the computer freezes instead of closing the tab, the handover can take longer than 10 seconds.

The room type and the sharing mode cannot be changed after the room is created. Multi-sharing rooms ("Create room (BETA)"), including the camera, are a beta feature.

The camera has no microphone, uses the device's default camera and cannot be picked from a list. Rooms created with "Share screen" have no camera.

Duplicate-tab detection only works within the same browser profile. Another browser, a private window or another device counts as a different viewer.

Bans after a kick are temporary and can be bypassed, for example with a VPN or a different network. The IP ban may also affect other people who share the same network.

If the host closes the tab, the room ends immediately. Joining another room as a viewer also closes your own room.

Automatic reconnection stops after 8 failed attempts, and the viewer then has to join again.


Privacy

No accounts, no analytics and no cookies.

The browser stores only your settings (language, theme, the automatic quality options and the notifications option), your nickname, a flag saying you already saw the what's new notice, and a random identifier used to detect duplicate tabs and to apply temporary bans. All of them stay on your device.

Because connections are peer-to-peer, the host and each viewer can see each other's IP address. Keep this in mind before sharing a room code publicly.

Camera: the browser asks for permission each time the site needs the camera, and the site only turns it on after the host has allowed it. The camera image goes directly to the other people in the room, with no microphone and no recording, and stops as soon as you turn it off, leave the room or the room ends. Before turning it on, check what appears behind you.

IP notice: before creating or joining a room for the first time, the site shows a message explaining what the public IP reveals (roughly the internet provider and the city or region, not the exact location) and how to protect yourself, for example with a VPN. It is shown once, after you click "I understand", and stays available at any time through the "About your IP" link in the footer.

Terms of use and reports: the footer has a "Terms of use" link (no mandatory acceptance) and a "Report misuse" button that opens a small window. The window lists, in collapsible groups, the people who were in your last two rooms (the current one and the previous one), including those who already left; this list is kept only in your browser and is never sent unless you choose a person and send the report. You pick the person from that list; the report then carries that person's identifier (a short code tied to the browser, which does not change when the nickname changes) and the nicknames they used before in the same room. Outside a room you type the nickname. You also write what happened and can tick an option to include the latest chat messages (nothing from the chat is sent unless you tick it). From there you can open the message in Gmail, open your email app, or copy the text and send it to the site owner by any email service. By default the report only exists in the email that you send. If the site owner enables the optional Firebase setup (see FIREBASE-SETUP.txt), a "Send report" button also appears and sends the report to a private database (after sending, the window shows only a green confirmation message and closes by itself), which the owner reads in the admin.html page after signing in with a Google account; there the owner can search, filter by status, group by reported person, write notes and delete reports. Clearing the browser data generates a new identifier. The terms text is a simple draft and was not reviewed by a lawyer.

The host's browser reads each viewer's IP address only to apply a temporary ban after a kick. It is kept in memory, is never displayed, is never sent to other people in the room (not even to the person who would take over as host) and is discarded when the room ends.

Chat is not recorded or saved on any server. The host's browser keeps the last 50 messages in memory so people who join later can see them, and everything is cleared when the room ends.

Only share content you have the right to share. Streaming protected material, such as movies or series, may violate the terms of the service that provides it.


